{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017iH1XnNJcZKpU1b6iUaf4G","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient experimental NMR Murday Cotts temperature dependence cm2/s\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"The dynamics of liquid 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium acetate measured with implanted-ion $^8$Li $\\\\beta$-NMR\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1907.03684\"},{\"title\":\"link.springer.com\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553\"},{\"title\":\"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/\"},{\"title\":\"ecs.confex.com\",\"url\":\"https://ecs.confex.com/ecs/imlb2016/webprogram/Paper76763.html\"},{\"title\":\"s00231 011 0874 9\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9\"},{\"title\":\"www.rsc.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.rsc.org/suppdata/d3/cp/d3cp04666d/d3cp04666d1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"explore.cnr.it\",\"url\":\"https://explore.cnr.it/resource/item/440695\"},{\"title\":\"link.aps.org\",\"url\":\"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevB.83.094302\"},{\"title\":\"Application Note 17\",\"url\":\"https://nmr.oxinst.com/assets/uploads/X-Pulse_App_Note_17_Measuring_diffusion_at_different_temperatures_using_NMR_with_pulsed_field_gradients_WEB.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"next.sciencegate.app\",\"url\":\"https://next.sciencegate.app/document/10.1557/opl.2012.1517\"}]\n\nThe search did not return the actual Murday and Cotts numbers, so I can't give you the diffusion coefficient values in cm²/s or their temperature fit.\n\n- **The original paper:** Reference lists cite it as Murday and Cotts, \"Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium,\" *J. Chem. Phys.* 48:4938–4945 (1968). Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium. J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945\n- **Modern comparison:** A 2012 study by Wang and Ni used an EAM potential to model liquid lithium self-diffusion. It reports that the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient increases with temperature increasing and decreases with pressure increasing, and that calculated self-diffusion coefficient is in good agreement with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere. It also gives an Arrhenius fit, but the snippet does not show its parameters.\n- **Gap:** The search results don't include the Murday and Cotts fit parameters. To get them, you'd need the full 1968 paper or the Wang and Ni (*Heat Mass Transfer* 48, 253–257) article, which likely reproduces the experimental comparison.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"38c2b3be-e29f-40aa-8a94-71de4b72e65a","uuid":"10716d2e-639b-45dc-97fb-014e745756f2","timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:57:11.994Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient experimental NMR Murday Cotts temperature dependence cm2/s","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01KecVEX77Z17GEDNEQPdXcZ","content":[{"title":"The dynamics of liquid 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium acetate measured with implanted-ion $^8$Li $\\beta$-NMR","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1907.03684"},{"title":"link.springer.com","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553"},{"title":"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium","url":"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/"},{"title":"ecs.confex.com","url":"https://ecs.confex.com/ecs/imlb2016/webprogram/Paper76763.html"},{"title":"s00231 011 0874 9","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9"},{"title":"www.rsc.org","url":"https://www.rsc.org/suppdata/d3/cp/d3cp04666d/d3cp04666d1.pdf"},{"title":"explore.cnr.it","url":"https://explore.cnr.it/resource/item/440695"},{"title":"link.aps.org","url":"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevB.83.094302"},{"title":"Application Note 17","url":"https://nmr.oxinst.com/assets/uploads/X-Pulse_App_Note_17_Measuring_diffusion_at_different_temperatures_using_NMR_with_pulsed_field_gradients_WEB.pdf"},{"title":"next.sciencegate.app","url":"https://next.sciencegate.app/document/10.1557/opl.2012.1517"}]},"The search did not return the actual Murday and Cotts numbers, so I can't give you the diffusion coefficient values in cm²/s or their temperature fit.\n\n- **The original paper:** Reference lists cite it as Murday and Cotts, \"Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium,\" *J. Chem. Phys.* 48:4938–4945 (1968). Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium. J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945\n- **Modern comparison:** A 2012 study by Wang and Ni used an EAM potential to model liquid lithium self-diffusion. It reports that the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient increases with temperature increasing and decreases with pressure increasing, and that calculated self-diffusion coefficient is in good agreement with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere. It also gives an Arrhenius fit, but the snippet does not show its parameters.\n- **Gap:** The search results don't include the Murday and Cotts fit parameters. To get them, you'd need the full 1968 paper or the Wang and Ni (*Heat Mass Transfer* 48, 253–257) article, which likely reproduces the experimental comparison."],"durationSeconds":6.288800632000002,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017iH1XnNJcZKpU1b6iUaf4G","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AkTPtyF6PCKAzs8ymy8w4F","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium density temperature correlation g/cm3 melting point 453.7 K\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"repo.qst.go.jp\",\"url\":\"https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902\"},{\"title\":\"LITHIUM (Li)\",\"url\":\"https://qedfusion.org/LIB/PROPS/PANOS/li.html\"},{\"title\":\"ntrs.nasa.gov\",\"url\":\"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19680018893/downloads/19680018893.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"repo.qst.go.jp\",\"url\":\"https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902/export/json\"},{\"title\":\"wulixb.iphy.ac.cn\",\"url\":\"https://wulixb.iphy.ac.cn/article/doi/10.7498/aps.55.76\"},{\"title\":\"Operational conditions:\",\"url\":\"https://www.chemeo.com/mid/22-277-a/Choline-chloride_Urea_lithium-chloride\"},{\"title\":\"Lithium chloride · LiCl\",\"url\":\"https://processconvert.pages.dev/substances/lithium-chloride\"},{\"title\":\"densite et temperature de cristallisation d une solution aqueuse de 22391\",\"url\":\"https://iifiir.org/fr/fridoc/densite-et-temperature-de-cristallisation-d-une-solution-aqueuse-de-22391\"},{\"title\":\"Contents - Index\",\"url\":\"https://fchartsoftware.com/ees/libr_help/hs400.htm\"},{\"title\":\"elogim.com\",\"url\":\"https://springerlink.bibliotecabuap.elogim.com/content/pdf/10.1361/105497198770341761.pdf\"}]\n\nThe search turned up one useful source for pure liquid lithium, plus a NASA report that gives the correlation approach but not the exact formula in the snippet.\n\n- **Pure liquid lithium correlation:** QEDfusion gives an empirical density fit for liquid lithium in g/cm3, with T* = (T - 271.7). The same page lists a melting point of 180.5 C, which matches 453.7 K.\n- **Temperature units:** The fit's offset only reproduces the page's own 20 °C density (0.534 g/cm3) if T is in °C. Plugging in 180.5 °C gives roughly 0.518 g/cm3 near the melting point. That value is my own calculation from the formula, not a figure from the source.\n- **NASA correlation:** A NASA report describes correlating liquid lithium properties, including density, using both experimental data and theoretical analyses. It favored a linear form for density, but the snippet does not show the coefficients or the units of T.\n- **Lead-lithium alloys:** A QST paper gives a density fit for liquid Pb–Li alloys, but it does not apply to pure lithium.\n\nI'd use the QEDfusion equation for pure liquid lithium near 454 K, but check the source's temperature units against the NASA report before relying on it.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"38c2b3be-e29f-40aa-8a94-71de4b72e65a","uuid":"915dbccb-8ca6-494c-8309-ab07dc3bf7bf","timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:57:13.675Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium density temperature correlation g/cm3 melting point 453.7 K","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01U6yPaNmo6GU2rCxqaV2aHe","content":[{"title":"repo.qst.go.jp","url":"https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902"},{"title":"LITHIUM (Li)","url":"https://qedfusion.org/LIB/PROPS/PANOS/li.html"},{"title":"ntrs.nasa.gov","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19680018893/downloads/19680018893.pdf"},{"title":"repo.qst.go.jp","url":"https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902/export/json"},{"title":"wulixb.iphy.ac.cn","url":"https://wulixb.iphy.ac.cn/article/doi/10.7498/aps.55.76"},{"title":"Operational conditions:","url":"https://www.chemeo.com/mid/22-277-a/Choline-chloride_Urea_lithium-chloride"},{"title":"Lithium chloride · LiCl","url":"https://processconvert.pages.dev/substances/lithium-chloride"},{"title":"densite et temperature de cristallisation d une solution aqueuse de 22391","url":"https://iifiir.org/fr/fridoc/densite-et-temperature-de-cristallisation-d-une-solution-aqueuse-de-22391"},{"title":"Contents - Index","url":"https://fchartsoftware.com/ees/libr_help/hs400.htm"},{"title":"elogim.com","url":"https://springerlink.bibliotecabuap.elogim.com/content/pdf/10.1361/105497198770341761.pdf"}]},"The search turned up one useful source for pure liquid lithium, plus a NASA report that gives the correlation approach but not the exact formula in the snippet.\n\n- **Pure liquid lithium correlation:** QEDfusion gives an empirical density fit for liquid lithium in g/cm3, with T* = (T - 271.7). The same page lists a melting point of 180.5 C, which matches 453.7 K.\n- **Temperature units:** The fit's offset only reproduces the page's own 20 °C density (0.534 g/cm3) if T is in °C. Plugging in 180.5 °C gives roughly 0.518 g/cm3 near the melting point. That value is my own calculation from the formula, not a figure from the source.\n- **NASA correlation:** A NASA report describes correlating liquid lithium properties, including density, using both experimental data and theoretical analyses. It favored a linear form for density, but the snippet does not show the coefficients or the units of T.\n- **Lead-lithium alloys:** A QST paper gives a density fit for liquid Pb–Li alloys, but it does not apply to pure lithium.\n\nI'd use the QEDfusion equation for pure liquid lithium near 454 K, but check the source's temperature units against the NASA report before relying on it."],"durationSeconds":7.383585788999997,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AkTPtyF6PCKAzs8ymy8w4F","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
